// Builtins.
//
// The catalogue was generated once from the running `wcl` binary — every
// signature, parameter and return description below is `fn_signature`'s
// own text for that builtin, read through `builtin_names()`. Every worked
// example was then executed (`wcl repl`, or `wcl get` against the files
// shown) and its printed answer pasted back. Hand-maintained from here:
// when a builtin changes, re-run its example and edit the entry.
page lang_builtins {
  title = "Builtins"

  h1 "Builtins"

  p "A **builtin** is a function the language already knows. You do not import it, declare it or define it. You call it. There are exactly one hundred, and the `wcl` CLI adds two more of wdoc's own, for the 102 this chapter counts."

  p "The bulk of this chapter is a catalogue. Each entry carries one builtin's signature, its parameters, its return value, and an example whose printed answer came from a real run."

  p "The five sections before the catalogue are the part you cannot get by looking one name up: how a call behaves, how to ask the language what it has, and which of the near-identical pairs to reach for."

  h2 "One call, one answer"

  p "A builtin call is an ordinary call expression. [Expressions and operators](lang_expressions#calls) covers the syntax. Four rules govern what a builtin does with it."

  list {
    li "**Arity is fixed.** Every builtin but one takes an exact number of arguments. The wrong argument count is an error at the call site, not a partially applied function. WCL has no currying."
    li "**Arguments are positional.** WCL has no keyword arguments and no defaults. A builtin that wants named options takes a record instead, so you write the option names at the call site."
    li "**Arguments are values.** WCL evaluates each one before the call, left to right. A function argument, the `fn(x: i64) -> i64 { … }` you hand to `map`, is a value like any other. See [Functions](lang_functions)."
    li "**A failure aborts the field.** A builtin that cannot answer reports an error rather than returning a sentinel, and that error propagates out through whichever field forced it. `try body catch e => fallback` recovers one. See [Control flow](lang_control_flow)."
  }

  p "The one exception to fixed arity is `format`, which takes a template plus as many arguments as the template has `{}` slots."

  callout "Builtins are the last place the resolver looks" {
    class = ["note"]
    body  = "The resolver checks a name in call position against the document first: `let` bindings, `fn` items and fields. Only then does it check the builtin registry. So a `fn len(xs: list<utf8>) -> u32 { … }` of your own **shadows** the builtin inside its scope. The flip side is the error message. A name that resolves to nothing callable is reported as `unknown built-in 'box'`, because the registry is where the search ended, not where it began."
  }

  h2 "A file that uses seven of them"

  p "Type this one and run it. It is a three-service fleet that reports on itself, and it calls `map`, `sort`, `unique`, `max_by`, `sum`, `len` and `format` to do it."

  code wcl {
    filename = "release.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
# release.wcl — one fleet, seven builtins.
@block("service")
type Service {
  @inline(0) name: utf8
  region:   utf8
  replicas: u32
}

@document
type Release {
  @children("service") services: list<Service>

  names:    list<utf8>
  regions:  list<utf8>
  busiest:  utf8
  replicas: u32
  summary:  utf8
}

names    = sort(map(services, fn(s: Service) -> utf8 { s.name }))
regions  = unique(map(services, fn(s: Service) -> utf8 { s.region }))
busiest  = { let s = max_by(services, fn(s: Service) -> u32 { s.replicas }); s.name }
replicas = sum(map(services, fn(s: Service) -> u32 { s.replicas }))
summary  = format("{} services over {} regions, {} replicas, busiest is {}",
                  len(services), len(regions), replicas, busiest)

service "web"   { region = "us-east-1"  replicas = 4u32 }
service "db"    { region = "us-east-1"  replicas = 2u32 }
service "cache" { region = "eu-west-1"  replicas = 1u32 }
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl check release.wcl
OK
$ wcl get release.wcl names
["cache", "db", "web"]
$ wcl get release.wcl regions
["us-east-1", "eu-west-1"]
$ wcl get release.wcl busiest
"web"
$ wcl get release.wcl replicas
7u32
$ wcl get release.wcl summary
"3 services over 2 regions, 7 replicas, busiest is web"
CONSOLE
  }

  p "Three things in that file are worth naming before the catalogue starts. A gather field is a `list<Service>`, so every list builtin works on it directly. `unique` keeps **first-seen** order, so `us-east-1` leads. And `busiest` wraps its call in a block expression: `max_by` returns a record-shaped value, and reading a field straight off a call, `max_by(…).name`, fails with `expected a reference, got call`. Bind it with a `let` first. That rule bites hardest in [Reflection](#reflection), where almost every builtin answers a record."

  h2 "Asking the language what it has"

  p "The catalogue below comes from the language itself, and you can regenerate it. `builtin_names()` answers every registered name, sorted, and `fn_signature(name)` answers that builtin's documentation as a record."

  code wcl {
    filename = "catalogue.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
@document
type Catalogue {
  count: usize
  first: list<utf8>
  sig:   utf8
}

count = len(builtin_names())
first = take(builtin_names(), 3)
sig   = { let s = fn_signature("map"); s.signature }
WCL
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get catalogue.wcl count
102
$ wcl get catalogue.wcl first
["__wdoc_slot", "abs", "acos"]
$ wcl get catalogue.wcl sig
"fn(xs: [T], f: fn (T) -> U) -> [U]"
CONSOLE
  }

  p "The count is 102 rather than 100, and the first name is one you did not write, because the environment a builtin lives in belongs to the **host**, not to the language. The `wcl` CLI is a wdoc-aware host: it registers wdoc's two builtins into every document it opens, whether or not the document imports wdoc. The bare language registers one hundred. `wcl repl` shows both counts. A REPL started with a file opens that file the way the CLI opens any document. A REPL started without a file evaluates against an empty document, in the plain language environment:"

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ echo 'len(builtin_names())' | wcl repl
100
$ echo 'len(builtin_names())' | wcl repl release.wcl
102
CONSOLE
  }

  p "Five modules of `wcl_lang` register the hundred. This chapter groups them by what they are for rather than by which module holds them."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Module" | "Registers" | "Covered in" |
      | "`collections`" | "54" | "[Lists](#lists), [Records](#records), [Strings](#strings), [Tensors](#tensors), [Failing on purpose](#failing-on-purpose)" |
      | "`math`" | "29" | "[Math](#math)" |
      | "`reflect`" | "12" | "[Reflection](#reflection)" |
      | "`paths`" | "3" | "[Paths and globs](#paths-and-globs)" |
      | "`units`" | "2" | "[Units](#units)" |
      | "wdoc, via the host" | "2" | "[Builtins the host adds](#builtins-the-host-adds)" |
  }

  h2 "Choosing between the near-twins"

  p "Several builtins answer nearly the same question, and picking the wrong one of a pair is the most common way to be surprised. This is the table you cannot build by reading either entry on its own."

  table {
    rows:
      | "When you want" | "Reach for" | "Not" | "Because" |
      | "A substring test" | "`contains(s, needle)`" | "`list_contains`" | "`contains` is strings only and _fails_ on a list. `list_contains` accepts a list or a tensor and answers `false` for anything else, silently" |
      | "The matching element" | "`find(xs, pred)`" | "`index_of` / `list_contains`" | "`find` answers the element or `none`, `index_of` answers a position or `-1`, `list_contains` answers a `bool`. Three questions, three shapes" |
      | "A sub-list by position" | "`slice(xs, a, b)`" | "`take` + `drop`" | "`slice` clamps both bounds and also reads a string. `take`/`drop` are the one-sided forms and are lists only" |
      | "The smallest of a list" | "`min_by(xs, key)`" | "`min(a, b)`" | "`min` compares exactly two numbers. `min_by` walks a list and answers an element, or `none` when it is empty" |
      | "A sorted list" | "`sort(xs)`" | "`sort_by(xs, key)`" | "`sort` orders numbers or strings directly and _fails_ on a mixed list. `sort_by` is for everything else, and is stable" |
      | "A string built from parts" | "`format(t, …)`" | "`concat` / `join`" | "`format` renders any value into a `{}` slot. `concat` and `join` demand strings. The interpolated `$\"…${x}…\"` literal renders the same way `format` does. See [Values and primitives](lang_values#interpolation)" |
      | "To stop on a bad value" | "`assert(cond, msg)`" | "`error` / `panic`" | "`assert` is the guard form. `error` and `panic` abort unconditionally. All three fail the same way, and `try`/`catch` recovers all three" |
      | "A byte size rendered" | "`format_unit(v, type, unit)`" | "`format_unit_value`" | "`format_unit` reads the factor from the type's `@unit` decorator, so it survives a change to the factor. `format_unit_value` takes the factor from you and checks nothing" |
      | "To know if a path is covered" | "`path_contains(p, c)`" | "`glob_match` / `glob_overlaps`" | "`path_contains` is a segment-aware prefix test between two concrete paths. `glob_match` tests one path against a pattern. `glob_overlaps` tests two _patterns_ against each other" |
  }

  p "One pair deserves its own sentence. `error` and `panic` are the **same function under two names**. They have identical implementations and identical `error: <msg>` output, and `try`/`catch` catches both. Nothing about `panic` is more final than `error`. Choose between them for the reader, not for the runtime."

  h2 "How to read an entry"

  p "Every entry below carries four things: the signature, a one-line description, a table of parameters and the return, and an example. The signature is the one the language reports for itself through `fn_signature`, which uses an **informal notation**. It is documentation, not a type you may write."

  table {
    rows:
      | "In a signature" | "Means" | "Legal as a field type" | "Legal as a parameter type" |
      | "`[T]`" | "A list of any one element type" | "No. Write `list<T>`. See [Lists, tensors and records](lang_collections#lists)" | "No. Write `list<T>`" |
      | "`T` / `U` / `K` / `A` / `B`" | "A type variable: whatever you pass, or whatever your callback returns" | "No. WCL has no generic declarations" | "No" |
      | "`number`" | "Any numeric value, widened to `f64` on the way in" | "No. Write `i64`, `u32`, `f64` or another concrete numeric type. See [Values and primitives](lang_values#numbers)" | "Yes" |
      | "`record`" | "A record value, or a union variant with a record body" | "No. Declare a type of your own. See [Lists, tensors and records](lang_collections#records)" | "Yes" |
      | "`&T`" | "A reference to a declaration: a type, field, block or variant named in an expression" | "Yes. See [Types](lang_types#reference-types)" | "Yes" |
      | "`any`" | "Any value at all" | "No" | "No" |
      | "`never`" | "The call does not return" | "No" | "No" |
  }

  p "Two consequences follow. Most of that notation is for reading, not for writing. A field declared `record` fails with `unknown type 'record'`. So the result of a builtin that answers a record usually stays inside an expression, which is where most of them belong. To store it in a field, copy it into a type you declared. A **callback parameter** is looser: it may be typed `record`, `number` or `&T`. That is why the reflection examples below write `fn(f: record) -> utf8 { … }`."

  p "And `[T]` says nothing about the element type. `sort` and `sum` check theirs at run time and fail on a mixed list, because the signature cannot check it for them."

  p "The example line ends in a `# → …` comment carrying the answer `wcl repl` prints for it. That is the display form: `abs(-7.5)` is `7.5` and not `7`, because every math builtin answers an `f64`."

  h2 "Lists"

  p "Twenty-eight builtins over `list<T>`, the collection everything else turns into. A gather field is one, a table's rows are one, and `split`, `range`, `keys` and `chars` all answer one. [Lists, tensors and records](lang_collections#lists) covers the value. This section covers what you can do to it."

  p "They divide by job. **Build** one with `range`. **Read** one with `len`, `at`, `head`, `tail`, `take`, `drop`, `slice`, `index_of` and `list_contains`. **Transform** one with `map`, `filter`, `fold`, `flatten`, `zip`, `enumerate`, `reverse` and `unique`. **Search** one with `any`, `all` and `find`. **Order** one with `sort`, `sort_by`, `sort_connected`, `min_by`, `max_by` and `group_by`. And `sum` reduces a numeric one."

  p "Nine of them also accept a **tensor**, reading its flat row-major data: `at`, `fold`, `head`, `index_of`, `len`, `list_contains`, `map`, `sum` and `tail`. Only `map` keeps the shape. The rest answer a plain value or a plain list. The other nineteen refuse a tensor, and `filter` says why: dropping elements would invalidate the shape."

  p "Two accept a **string**: `len`, which counts characters, and `slice`. Everything else that reads a string lives under [Strings](#strings)."

  h3 "all"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], pred: fn (T) -> bool) -> bool
SRC
  }

  p "`true` when the predicate holds for every element (short-circuits; `true` for an empty list)."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to test." |
      | "`pred`" | "`fn (T) -> bool`" | "Predicate applied to each element." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`bool`" | "`true` if every element satisfies the predicate." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
all([2, 4, 6], fn(x: i64) -> bool { x % 2 == 0 })   # → true
SRC
  }

  h3 "any"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], pred: fn (T) -> bool) -> bool
SRC
  }

  p "`true` when the predicate holds for at least one element (short-circuits)."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to test." |
      | "`pred`" | "`fn (T) -> bool`" | "Predicate applied to each element." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`bool`" | "`true` if any element satisfies the predicate." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
any([1, 2, 3], fn(x: i64) -> bool { x > 2 })   # → true
SRC
  }

  h3 "at"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], i: i64) -> T
SRC
  }

  p "The element at a zero-based index; errors if out of bounds or negative."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to index." |
      | "`i`" | "`i64`" | "The zero-based index." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`T`" | "The element at `i`." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
at([10, 20, 30], 1)   # → 20
SRC
  }

  p "Out of range is a **failure**, not a `none`: `at([1, 2], 5)` reports `'at': at: index 5 out of bounds`. When the index may not be there, reach for [`head`](#head) or a guarded [`slice`](#slice). Also accepts a tensor, indexing its flat data."

  h3 "drop"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], n: i64) -> [T]
SRC
  }

  p "Every element of a list after the first `n`."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to drop from." |
      | "`n`" | "`i64`" | "How many leading elements to skip." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[T]`" | "The elements after the first `n`." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
drop([1, 2, 3, 4], 2)   # → [3, 4]
SRC
  }

  h3 "enumerate"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T]) -> [[i64, T]]
SRC
  }

  p "Pair every element with its zero-based index, as `[index, element]` pairs."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to enumerate." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[[i64, T]]`" | "`[index, element]` pairs." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
enumerate(["a", "b"])   # → [[0, "a"], [1, "b"]]
SRC
  }

  p "The pairs are two-element **lists**, not records. Read the index with `at(pair, 0)` and the element with `at(pair, 1)`."

  h3 "filter"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], pred: fn (T) -> bool) -> [T]
SRC
  }

  p "Keep only the list elements for which the predicate returns `true`."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to filter." |
      | "`pred`" | "`fn (T) -> bool`" | "Predicate deciding whether to keep an element." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[T]`" | "The elements for which the predicate returned `true`." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
filter([1, 2, 3, 4], fn(x: i64) -> bool { x % 2 == 0 })   # → [2, 4]
SRC
  }

  p "Refuses a tensor outright, because dropping elements would invalidate the shape. The message says so: `filter: tensors are not supported (would invalidate shape); use map or convert to a list`."

  h3 "find"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], pred: fn (T) -> bool) -> T
SRC
  }

  p "The first element for which the predicate returns `true`, or `none`."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to search." |
      | "`pred`" | "`fn (T) -> bool`" | "Predicate applied to each element." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`T`" | "The first matching element, or `none`." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
find([1, 2, 3], fn(x: i64) -> bool { x > 1 })   # → 2
SRC
  }

  h3 "flatten"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xss: [[T]]) -> [T]
SRC
  }

  p "Concatenate a list of lists into a single list, one level deep."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xss`" | "`[[T]]`" | "A list whose elements are themselves lists." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[T]`" | "The inner lists concatenated, one level deep." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
flatten([[1, 2], [3]])   # → [1, 2, 3]
SRC
  }

  h3 "fold"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], init: U, f: fn (U, T) -> U) -> U
SRC
  }

  p "Reduce a list or tensor to a single value by repeatedly combining the accumulator with each element."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list or tensor to reduce." |
      | "`init`" | "`U`" | "The initial accumulator value." |
      | "`f`" | "`fn (U, T) -> U`" | "Combines the accumulator with the next element." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`U`" | "The final accumulator value." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
fold([1, 2, 3], 0, fn(acc: i64, x: i64) -> i64 { acc + x })   # → 6
SRC
  }

  p "Accepts a tensor, reading its flat row-major data."

  h3 "group_by"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], key: fn (T) -> K) -> [record]
SRC
  }

  p "Group elements by a key function into `{ key, items }` records, in first-seen key order."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to group." |
      | "`key`" | "`fn (T) -> K`" | "Maps each element to its group key." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[record]`" | "One `{ key, items }` record per distinct key." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
group_by([1, 2, 3, 4], fn(x: i64) -> i64 { x % 2 })   # → [ { items: [1, 3], key: 1 },  { items: [2, 4], key: 0 }]
SRC
  }

  p "Groups appear in **first-seen key order**, not sorted key order. Grouping `[1, 2, 3, 4]` by `x % 2` puts the odd group first, because `1` came first."

  h3 "head"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T]) -> T
SRC
  }

  p "The first element of a list or tensor (`none` when empty)."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "A list or tensor." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`T`" | "The first element, or `none` if empty." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
head([1, 2, 3])   # → 1
SRC
  }

  p "The one list reader that answers `none` instead of failing on an empty list. It also accepts a tensor, and reads the first element of its flat data."

  h3 "index_of"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], needle: T) -> i64
SRC
  }

  p "The index of the first element equal to `needle`, or `-1` if absent."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to search." |
      | "`needle`" | "`T`" | "The value to look for." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`i64`" | "The zero-based index, or `-1` if not found." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
index_of([10, 20, 30], 20)   # → 1
SRC
  }

  p "Accepts a list or a tensor, and answers `-1` for anything else, including a string, which it never searches."

  h3 "len"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T]) -> usize
SRC
  }

  p "The number of elements in a list or tensor, or characters in a string."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "A list, tensor, or string." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`usize`" | "The number of elements (or characters)." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
len([10, 20, 30])   # → 3
SRC
  }

  p "Three shapes, one question. A list answers with its element count, a tensor with the product of its dimensions, and a string with its **character** count. `len(\"héllo\")` is `5`, not the byte length."

  h3 "list_contains"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], needle: T) -> bool
SRC
  }

  p "Whether a list contains a value equal to `needle`."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to search." |
      | "`needle`" | "`T`" | "The value to look for." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`bool`" | "`true` if an equal element is present." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
list_contains([1, 2, 3], 2)   # → true
SRC
  }

  p "Accepts a list or a tensor. Anything else answers `false` rather than failing, so a mis-typed argument is silent. See [Choosing between the near-twins](#choosing-between-the-near-twins)."

  h3 "map"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], f: fn (T) -> U) -> [U]
SRC
  }

  p "Apply a function to every element of a list or tensor, returning the transformed collection."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list or tensor to transform." |
      | "`f`" | "`fn (T) -> U`" | "Function applied to each element." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[U]`" | "A new collection of the transformed elements." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
map([1, 2, 3], fn(x: i64) -> i64 { x * 2 })   # → [2, 4, 6]
SRC
  }

  p "The only iterator that keeps a tensor's shape: `map` over a `tensor[2x2]` answers a `tensor[2x2]`. Every other transformer either refuses a tensor or flattens it."

  h3 "max_by"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], key: fn (T) -> K) -> T
SRC
  }

  p "The element with the largest key, or `none` for an empty list."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to search." |
      | "`key`" | "`fn (T) -> K`" | "Maps each element to its comparison key." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`T`" | "The element with the largest key, or `none`." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
max_by(["a", "abc", "ab"], fn(s: utf8) -> i64 { len(s) })   # → "abc"
SRC
  }

  h3 "min_by"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], key: fn (T) -> K) -> T
SRC
  }

  p "The element with the smallest key, or `none` for an empty list."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to search." |
      | "`key`" | "`fn (T) -> K`" | "Maps each element to its comparison key." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`T`" | "The element with the smallest key, or `none`." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
min_by(["abc", "a", "ab"], fn(s: utf8) -> i64 { len(s) })   # → "a"
SRC
  }

  h3 "range"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(start: i64, end: i64) -> [i64]
SRC
  }

  p "The half-open integer range `[start, end)` as a list."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`start`" | "`i64`" | "Inclusive lower bound." |
      | "`end`" | "`i64`" | "Exclusive upper bound; must be >= `start`." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[i64]`" | "The integers from `start` up to (but excluding) `end`." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
range(0, 4)   # → [0, 1, 2, 3]
SRC
  }

  h3 "reverse"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T]) -> [T]
SRC
  }

  p "Reverse the order of a list's elements."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to reverse." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[T]`" | "The list in reverse order." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
reverse([1, 2, 3])   # → [3, 2, 1]
SRC
  }

  h3 "slice"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: utf8 | [T], start: i64, end: i64) -> utf8 | [T]
SRC
  }

  p "The half-open range `[start, end)` of a string's characters or a list's elements (bounds are clamped)."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`utf8 | [T]`" | "The string or list to slice." |
      | "`start`" | "`i64`" | "Inclusive start index (clamped to the length)." |
      | "`end`" | "`i64`" | "Exclusive end index (clamped to the length)." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8 | [T]`" | "The sub-string / sub-list." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
slice([10, 20, 30, 40], 1, 3)   # → [20, 30]
SRC
  }

  p "The one slicer that reads a string as well as a list. Both bounds are clamped, so `slice([1, 2, 3], 0, 99)` is the whole list rather than an error."

  h3 "sort"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T]) -> [T]
SRC
  }

  p "Sort a list — numerically for all-numeric lists, lexicographically for all-string lists."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "An all-numeric or all-string list." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[T]`" | "The sorted list." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
sort([3, 1, 2])   # → [1, 2, 3]
SRC
  }

  p "Mixed lists fail loudly rather than inventing an order: `sort([1, \"a\"])` reports `list must be all numeric or all strings, found mixed`. Tensors are refused. Sort `tensor_data(t)` instead."

  h3 "sort_by"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], key: fn (T) -> K) -> [T]
SRC
  }

  p "Sort a list by a key function (stable). Keys must be all numeric or all strings."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to sort." |
      | "`key`" | "`fn (T) -> K`" | "Maps each element to its sort key." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[T]`" | "The elements ordered by ascending key." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
sort_by(["abc", "a", "ab"], fn(s: utf8) -> i64 { len(s) })   # → ["a", "ab", "abc"]
SRC
  }

  h3 "sort_connected"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(items: [T], edges: [{source, destination, ...}]) -> [T]
SRC
  }

  p "Reorder a list so that items joined by edges cluster together (recursing into `children`)."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`items`" | "`[T]`" | "Items identified by an `id` field (possibly nested via `children`)." |
      | "`edges`" | "`[{source, destination, ...}]`" | "Edge records linking item ids." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[T]`" | "The reordered list, connected items adjacent." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
sort_connected([{ id: "a" }, { id: "b" }, { id: "c" }],
               [{ source: "a", destination: "c" }])   # → [ { id: "a" },  { id: "c" },  { id: "b" }]
SRC
  }

  p "Written for diagram layout. It reads an `id` field off each item, recurses into a `children` field, and reorders so that edge-joined items sit together. Items that no edge names keep their relative order."

  h3 "sum"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [number]) -> number
SRC
  }

  p "Add together every element of a non-empty homogeneous numeric list or tensor."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[number]`" | "A non-empty list or tensor of one numeric type." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`number`" | "The total, in the element's numeric type." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
sum([1, 2, 3, 4])   # → 10
SRC
  }

  p "An empty list is a failure, because there is no zero to return without knowing the element type. `sum([])` reports `'sum': sum: empty list`. Also accepts a tensor."

  h3 "tail"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T]) -> [T]
SRC
  }

  p "Every element of a list or tensor except the first."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "A list or tensor." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[T]`" | "The elements after the first." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
tail([1, 2, 3])   # → [2, 3]
SRC
  }

  p "Accepts a tensor, and drops the shape. The answer is always a plain list."

  h3 "take"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T], n: i64) -> [T]
SRC
  }

  p "The first `n` elements of a list (fewer if the list is shorter)."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to take from." |
      | "`n`" | "`i64`" | "How many leading elements to keep." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[T]`" | "The first `n` elements." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
take([1, 2, 3, 4], 2)   # → [1, 2]
SRC
  }

  h3 "unique"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(xs: [T]) -> [T]
SRC
  }

  p "Remove duplicate elements from a list, keeping first-seen order."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`xs`" | "`[T]`" | "The list to deduplicate." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[T]`" | "The list with duplicates removed." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
unique([1, 2, 2, 3, 1])   # → [1, 2, 3]
SRC
  }

  h3 "zip"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(a: [A], b: [B]) -> [(A, B)]
SRC
  }

  p "Pair up elements of two lists by index, stopping at the shorter length."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`a`" | "`[A]`" | "The first list." |
      | "`b`" | "`[B]`" | "The second list." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[(A, B)]`" | "Index-paired `[a, b]` lists, up to the shorter length." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
zip([1, 2, 3], ["a", "b"])   # → [[1, "a"], [2, "b"]]
SRC
  }

  p "The pairs are two-element lists, exactly as [`enumerate`](#enumerate) produces."

  h2 "Records"

  p "Four builtins over the record, the `{ name: value, … }` literal covered in [Lists, tensors and records](lang_collections#records). A record has no declared type and no field order, so `keys` sorts."

  h3 "keys"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(r: record) -> [utf8]
SRC
  }

  p "The field names of a record, in deterministic (sorted) order."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`r`" | "`record`" | "A record value (or a union variant with a record body)." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[utf8]`" | "The field names." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
keys({ name: "Rex", age: 4 })   # → ["age", "name"]
SRC
  }

  p "Sorted, always. WCL does not preserve a record's field order, so `keys` gives you a deterministic one instead."

  h3 "map_values"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(r: record, f: fn (T) -> U) -> record
SRC
  }

  p "Apply a function to every field value of a record, keeping the keys."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`r`" | "`record`" | "The record to transform." |
      | "`f`" | "`fn (T) -> U`" | "Function applied to each field value." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`record`" | "A record with the same keys and transformed values." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
map_values({ low: 1, high: 9 }, fn(x: i64) -> i64 { x * 2 })   # →  { high: 18, low: 2 }
SRC
  }

  h3 "merge"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(a: record, b: record) -> record
SRC
  }

  p "Combine two records into one; fields of `b` win on a name clash."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`a`" | "`record`" | "The base record." |
      | "`b`" | "`record`" | "The overriding record." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`record`" | "A record with the union of both field sets." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
merge({ host: "localhost", port: 80 }, { port: 8080 })   # →  { host: "localhost", port: 8080 }
SRC
  }

  h3 "values"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(r: record) -> [T]
SRC
  }

  p "The field values of a record, in the same order as `keys`."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`r`" | "`record`" | "A record value (or a union variant with a record body)." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[T]`" | "The field values." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
values({ name: "Rex", age: 4 })   # → [4, "Rex"]
SRC
  }

  p "In the same order as [`keys`](#keys), which is sorted by field name. `values({ name: \"Rex\", age: 4 })` is `[4, \"Rex\"]`, not `[\"Rex\", 4]`."

  h2 "Strings"

  p "Fifteen builtins over `utf8`. Every one of them counts in **characters**, not bytes: `chars`, `pad_start` and `pad_end` all treat a multi-byte character as one unit."

  p "`len` and `slice` also read strings, but they are list builtins that happen to accept one, so they are catalogued under [Lists](#lists). `format_unit` and `format_unit_value` build strings too, and are under [Units](#units) because that is what they are about."

  h3 "chars"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(s: utf8) -> [utf8]
SRC
  }

  p "The characters of a string as a list of one-character strings."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`s`" | "`utf8`" | "The string to split into characters." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[utf8]`" | "One string per character." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
chars("abc")   # → ["a", "b", "c"]
SRC
  }

  p "Characters, not bytes: `chars(\"héllo\")` is five one-character strings."

  h3 "concat"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(a: utf8, b: utf8) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "Concatenate two strings into one."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`a`" | "`utf8`" | "The left-hand string." |
      | "`b`" | "`utf8`" | "The string appended after `a`." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "The two strings joined together." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
concat("foo", "bar")   # → "foobar"
SRC
  }

  h3 "contains"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(s: utf8, needle: utf8) -> bool
SRC
  }

  p "Whether a string contains a substring."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`s`" | "`utf8`" | "The string to search." |
      | "`needle`" | "`utf8`" | "The substring to look for." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`bool`" | "`true` if the substring is present." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
contains("hello", "ell")   # → true
SRC
  }

  p "Strings only. Its list-shaped twin is [`list_contains`](#list-contains), and passing a list here fails with `'contains': expected utf8 string, found list`."

  h3 "ends_with"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(s: utf8, suffix: utf8) -> bool
SRC
  }

  p "Whether a string ends with a suffix."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`s`" | "`utf8`" | "The string to test." |
      | "`suffix`" | "`utf8`" | "The suffix to look for." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`bool`" | "`true` if the string ends with the suffix." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
ends_with("hello", "lo")   # → true
SRC
  }

  h3 "format"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn (utf8, ...args) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "Substitute trailing arguments into a template's `{}` placeholders (`{{`/`}}` are literal braces)."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`template`" | "`utf8`" | "Template string with `{}` placeholders." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "The template with placeholders substituted." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
format("{} = {}", "x", 42)   # → "x = 42"
SRC
  }

  p "The one variadic builtin. `{}` takes the next argument in order. `{{` and `}}` are literal braces. A slot renders the value the way a person reads it, so a `u32` loses its suffix. That is the same rendering an interpolated `$\"…${x}…\"` string performs. See [Values and primitives](lang_values#interpolation)."

  h3 "join"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(parts: [utf8], sep: utf8) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "Join a list of strings into one, inserting a separator between each."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`parts`" | "`[utf8]`" | "The strings to join." |
      | "`sep`" | "`utf8`" | "The separator inserted between parts." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "The joined string." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
join(["a", "b", "c"], "-")   # → "a-b-c"
SRC
  }

  h3 "pad_end"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(s: utf8, width: i64, pad: utf8) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "Right-pad a string with a fill pattern until it is `width` characters long."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`s`" | "`utf8`" | "The string to pad." |
      | "`width`" | "`i64`" | "The target character count." |
      | "`pad`" | "`utf8`" | "The fill pattern (repeated / truncated as needed)." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "The padded string (unchanged if already wide enough)." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
pad_end("42", 5, "0")   # → "42000"
SRC
  }

  p "Same fill rule as [`pad_start`](#pad-start), applied on the right."

  h3 "pad_start"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(s: utf8, width: i64, pad: utf8) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "Left-pad a string with a fill pattern until it is `width` characters long."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`s`" | "`utf8`" | "The string to pad." |
      | "`width`" | "`i64`" | "The target character count." |
      | "`pad`" | "`utf8`" | "The fill pattern (repeated / truncated as needed)." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "The padded string (unchanged if already wide enough)." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
pad_start("42", 5, "0")   # → "00042"
SRC
  }

  p "`pad_start` repeats and truncates the pad pattern to fill the gap, so a multi-character pattern may end mid-pattern. A string already `width` wide comes back unchanged. Nothing is ever cut."

  h3 "repeat"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(s: utf8, n: i64) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "A string repeated `n` times (empty for `n <= 0`)."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`s`" | "`utf8`" | "The string to repeat." |
      | "`n`" | "`i64`" | "How many copies to concatenate." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "`n` copies of `s`." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
repeat("ab", 3)   # → "ababab"
SRC
  }

  p "`n <= 0` answers the empty string rather than failing."

  h3 "replace"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(s: utf8, old: utf8, new: utf8) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "Replace every occurrence of a substring with another."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`s`" | "`utf8`" | "The string to search." |
      | "`old`" | "`utf8`" | "The substring to find." |
      | "`new`" | "`utf8`" | "The replacement substring." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "The string with every match replaced." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
replace("hello world", "world", "there")   # → "hello there"
SRC
  }

  h3 "split"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(s: utf8, sep: utf8) -> [utf8]
SRC
  }

  p "Split a string on every occurrence of a separator into a list of pieces."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`s`" | "`utf8`" | "The string to split." |
      | "`sep`" | "`utf8`" | "The separator to split on." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[utf8]`" | "The pieces between separators." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
split("a,b,c", ",")   # → ["a", "b", "c"]
SRC
  }

  h3 "starts_with"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(s: utf8, prefix: utf8) -> bool
SRC
  }

  p "Whether a string begins with a prefix."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`s`" | "`utf8`" | "The string to test." |
      | "`prefix`" | "`utf8`" | "The prefix to look for." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`bool`" | "`true` if the string starts with the prefix." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
starts_with("hello", "he")   # → true
SRC
  }

  h3 "to_lower"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(s: utf8) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "Lowercase every character of a string."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`s`" | "`utf8`" | "The string to lowercase." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "The lowercased string." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
to_lower("AbC")   # → "abc"
SRC
  }

  h3 "to_upper"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(s: utf8) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "Uppercase every character of a string."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`s`" | "`utf8`" | "The string to uppercase." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "The uppercased string." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
to_upper("abc")   # → "ABC"
SRC
  }

  h3 "trim"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(s: utf8) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "Remove leading and trailing whitespace from a string."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`s`" | "`utf8`" | "The string to trim." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "The string without leading/trailing whitespace." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
trim("  hi  ")   # → "hi"
SRC
  }

  h2 "Tensors"

  p "One constructor and three accessors. A tensor is a flat, row-major buffer plus a shape. See [Lists, tensors and records](lang_collections#tensors). These four are the only builtins that know about the shape. The nine list builtins that accept a tensor all read its flat data, and only `map` gives a tensor back."

  h3 "tensor"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(data: [number], shape: [usize]) -> tensor<T>
SRC
  }

  p "Build a tensor from flat row-major data and a shape; the data length must equal the product of the dimensions."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`data`" | "`[number]`" | "Flat, row-major element data." |
      | "`shape`" | "`[usize]`" | "The dimension sizes." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`tensor<T>`" | "The constructed tensor." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
tensor([1, 2, 3, 4], [2, 2])   # → tensor[2x2](1, 2, 3, 4)
SRC
  }

  p "The data length must equal the product of the dimensions. `tensor([1, 2, 3], [2, 2])` reports `tensor: data length 3 does not match shape product 4`."

  h3 "tensor_data"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(t: tensor<T>) -> [T]
SRC
  }

  p "The flat row-major element data of a tensor as a list."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`t`" | "`tensor<T>`" | "The tensor to read." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[T]`" | "The tensor's flat, row-major element data." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
tensor_data(tensor([1, 2, 3, 4], [2, 2]))   # → [1, 2, 3, 4]
SRC
  }

  h3 "tensor_reshape"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(t: tensor<T>, shape: [usize]) -> tensor<T>
SRC
  }

  p "Reinterpret a tensor's data under a new shape; the element count must be unchanged."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`t`" | "`tensor<T>`" | "The tensor to reshape." |
      | "`shape`" | "`[usize]`" | "The new dimension sizes." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`tensor<T>`" | "The same data under the new shape." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
tensor_reshape(tensor([1, 2, 3, 4], [2, 2]), [4])   # → tensor[4](1, 2, 3, 4)
SRC
  }

  p "The element count must be unchanged. `tensor_reshape` does not copy or reorder the data. It only re-reads it."

  h3 "tensor_shape"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(t: tensor<T>) -> [usize]
SRC
  }

  p "The dimension sizes of a tensor as a list."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`t`" | "`tensor<T>`" | "The tensor to read." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[usize]`" | "The tensor's dimension sizes." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
tensor_shape(tensor([1, 2, 3, 4], [2, 2]))   # → [2, 2]
SRC
  }

  h2 "Failing on purpose"

  p "Three ways to stop. All three are the same abort: evaluation of the field that forced them ends, and the CLI prints the message after `error:`. `try body catch e => fallback` recovers all three. See [Control flow](lang_control_flow)."

  p "The difference is the shape of the call, not the severity. `assert` takes a condition and does nothing when it holds. `error` and `panic` take no condition and always abort. They are literally the same function registered twice."

  h3 "assert"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(cond: bool, msg: utf8) -> none
SRC
  }

  p "Return `none` when `cond` is true, otherwise abort with `msg`."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`cond`" | "`bool`" | "The condition that must hold." |
      | "`msg`" | "`utf8`" | "The error message reported when `cond` is false." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`none`" | "`none` when the assertion holds (otherwise aborts)." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
assert(1 + 1 == 2, "math is broken")   # → none
SRC
  }

  p "The return value is `none`, which is rarely what you want on the right of an `=`. Use it as a guard inside a block expression: `{ let _ = assert(self.port > 1024u32, \"port must be above 1024\"); 3u32 }`."

  h3 "error"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(msg: utf8) -> never
SRC
  }

  p "Abort evaluation with an error message."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`msg`" | "`utf8`" | "The error message to report." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`never`" | "Never returns — aborts evaluation." |
  }

  code wcl {
    filename = "guard.wcl"
    source = <<'SRC'
@block("service")
type Service {
  @inline(0) name: utf8
  port:    u32
  checked: u32
}

@document
type Cfg {
  @children("service") services: list<Service>
}

service "web" {
  port    = 80u32
  checked = if self.port > 1024u32 { self.port } else { error("port must be above 1024") }
}
SRC
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get guard.wcl services.web.checked
wcl::eval::user_error

  × error: port must be above 1024
CONSOLE
  }

  p "The failure aborts the field that called it, and the CLI prints the message verbatim after `error:`. `try … catch e => …` recovers it. See [Control flow](lang_control_flow)."

  h3 "panic"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(msg: utf8) -> never
SRC
  }

  p "Abort evaluation with an unrecoverable failure message."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`msg`" | "`utf8`" | "The failure message to report." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`never`" | "Never returns — aborts evaluation." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
panic("unreachable: two roots")   # → aborts: error: unreachable: two roots
SRC
  }

  p "Identical to [`error`](#error) in every observable way: same abort, same `error: <msg>` output, and equally catchable by `try`/`catch`. The two names differ in intent only."

  h2 "Math"

  p "Twenty-nine builtins, and one rule that covers all of them: **arguments widen to `f64` and results are `f64`**. `sqrt(144)` answers `12.0`, not `12`. That is why the signatures say `number` going in and `f64` coming out. An `i64`, a `u32` and an `f64` are all accepted, and none of them survives the call as itself."

  p "Nothing here is a constraint check. `sqrt(-1)`, `ln(0)` and `0.0 / 0.0` produce the IEEE answers (`NaN`, `-inf`) rather than errors, exactly as the floating-point arithmetic in [Expressions and operators](lang_expressions#integers-refuse-to-be-wrong-but-floats-do-not) does."

  p "The trigonometric functions work in **radians**. `radians` and `degrees` convert. `pi`, `tau` and `e` are the three nullary constants."

  h3 "abs"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(x: number) -> f64
SRC
