  h2 "Reflection"

  p "Twelve builtins that read the document rather than its data: what a type declares, what decorates it, what a namespace holds, what the source said. They exist so a document can generate its own reference. That is how the catalogue in this chapter was produced."

  p "Most of them take a **reference**, the declaration's bare name written in expression position. The rest take a name as a string. Every example below is a field of one small file, so you can see what each answers against the same schema:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "schema.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
# schema.wcl — a tiny schema that documents itself.
namespace app

# The edge service: terminates TLS and forwards to the app tier.
# Owned by the platform team.
@block("service")
type Service {
  @inline(0) name: identifier
  port: u32
  @child("limits") limits: Limits?
}

@block("limits")
type Limits {
  cpu: f64
}

@document
type Inventory {
  @children("service") services: list<Service>

  decorators:   list<utf8>
  block_kind:   utf8
  fields:       list<utf8>
  children:     list<utf8>
  declarations: list<utf8>
  legal_on:     usize
  kind:         utf8
  doc:          utf8
  source:       utf8
  map_sig:      utf8
  builtins:     usize
  doubled:      i64
}

decorators   = decorator_names(Service)
block_kind   = decorator_arg(Service, "block", "name")
fields       = map(type_fields(Service), fn(f: record) -> utf8 { f.name })
children     = map(child_types(Inventory), fn(t: &Service) -> utf8 { { let d = decl_info(t); d.name } })
declarations = map(namespace_decls("app"), fn(t: &Service) -> utf8 { { let d = decl_info(t); d.name } })
legal_on     = len(decorators_for_kind("service"))
kind         = { let d = decl_info(Service); d.kind }
doc          = doc_comment(Service)
source       = ast_string(Limits)
map_sig      = { let s = fn_signature("map"); s.signature }
builtins     = len(builtin_names())
doubled      = eval("2 * 21")

service web { port = 8080u32 }
WCL
  }

  p "Two shapes recur. A builtin returning `[&T]` answers a list of further references, so the usual move is to `map` over it and reflect each one. That is what `children` and `declarations` do above. And a builtin returning a `record` must be **bound before a field is read off it**: `decl_info(Service).kind` fails with `expected a reference, got call`, while `{ let d = decl_info(Service); d.kind }` answers `\"type\"`. Only `wcl get` and a build catch it. The field is lazy, so `wcl check` alone reports `OK`."

  h3 "ast_string"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(target: &T) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "Pretty-print the canonical source behind a reference (type/interface/union/symbol_set/block/field) or a function value."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`target`" | "`&T`" | "A dataref to a declaration, or a function value." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "The canonical (pretty-printed) source text." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
source = ast_string(Limits)
SRC
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get schema.wcl source
"@block(\"limits\")\ntype Limits {\n  cpu: f64\n}"
CONSOLE
  }

  h3 "builtin_names"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn() -> [utf8]
SRC
  }

  p "The names of every registered built-in function, sorted. Pair with `fn_signature` to introspect each one."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[utf8]`" | "Every built-in's name, sorted alphabetically." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
builtins = len(builtin_names())
SRC
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get schema.wcl builtins
102
CONSOLE
  }

  h3 "child_types"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(target: &T) -> [&T]
SRC
  }

  p "Reflect a type into references to the element types of its `@child` / `@children` block slots (own slots first, then inherited via `extends`). Pair with `type_table` / `type_fields` to auto-document the blocks a `@document` declares."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`target`" | "`&T`" | "A reference to a type or interface declaration." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[&T]`" | "One type reference per block slot. Slots that accept a union or interface resolve to that type's name; scalar (non-block) fields are skipped." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
children = map(child_types(Inventory), fn(t: &Service) -> utf8 { { let d = decl_info(t); d.name } })
SRC
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get schema.wcl children
["Service"]
CONSOLE
  }

  h3 "decl_info"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(target: &T) -> record
SRC
  }

  p "Describe a top-level declaration: its name, kind, doc comment, and schema classification (block / table / decorator / document)."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`target`" | "`&T`" | "A reference to a type, interface, union, or symbol_set declaration." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`record`" | "`{ name, full_name, kind, doc, is_imported, is_document, block_kind, table_kind, decorator_name, extends }`. The classification fields are `none` when the decorator is absent." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
kind = { let d = decl_info(Service); d.kind }
SRC
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get schema.wcl kind
"type"
CONSOLE
  }

  p "The `kind` of a `@document` type is still `\"type\"`. The `is_document` flag is what tells you it is the document schema."

  h3 "decorator_arg"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(target: &T, decorator: utf8, slot: utf8) -> any
SRC
  }

  p "Read one named argument of a decorator on a referenced declaration (`none` if absent)."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`target`" | "`&T`" | "A reference to a type, field, block, or variant." |
      | "`decorator`" | "`utf8`" | "The decorator name, e.g. `\"doc\"`." |
      | "`slot`" | "`utf8`" | "The argument (slot) name to read." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`any`" | "The argument's value, or `none` if absent." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
block_kind = decorator_arg(Service, "block", "name")
SRC
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get schema.wcl block_kind
"service"
CONSOLE
  }

  h3 "decorator_names"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(target: &T) -> [utf8]
SRC
  }

  p "List the names of the decorators attached to a referenced declaration."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`target`" | "`&T`" | "A reference to a type, field, block, or variant." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[utf8]`" | "The decorator names, in source order." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
decorators = decorator_names(Service)
SRC
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get schema.wcl decorators
["block"]
CONSOLE
  }

  h3 "decorators_for_kind"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(kind: utf8) -> [&T]
SRC
  }

  p "List references to decorator schemas applicable to a block kind. Pair with `decl_info`, `doc_comment`, and `type_fields` to render them."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`kind`" | "`utf8`" | "The block kind as a string; instance-derived kinds are supported." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[&T]`" | "One reference per applicable decorator-schema declaration." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
legal_on = len(decorators_for_kind("service"))
SRC
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get schema.wcl legal_on
22
CONSOLE
  }

  p "Answers the decorator **schemas** that are legal on the kind, every built-in one included. An ordinary block has 22, before the document declares any of its own."

  h3 "doc_comment"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(target: &T) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "The doc comment — the contiguous run of `#` / `//` lines immediately above a declaration — attached to a reference, or `\"\"` when there is none. Complements `decorator_arg(x, \"doc\", …)` for `@doc(\"…\")` metadata."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`target`" | "`&T`" | "A reference to a type, interface, union, variant, symbol_set, or field." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "The joined comment text, or `\"\"` when absent." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
doc = doc_comment(Service)
SRC
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get schema.wcl doc
"The edge service: terminates TLS and forwards to the app tier.\nOwned by the platform team."
CONSOLE
  }

  p "The run must be unbroken. A blank line ends it, and anything above the blank line belongs to nothing. See [Documents, fields and blocks](lang_documents#doc-comments)."

  h3 "eval"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(src: utf8) -> any
SRC
  }

  p "Parse a string as a WCL expression and evaluate it in the current scope."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`src`" | "`utf8`" | "WCL expression source to parse and evaluate." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`any`" | "The value the expression evaluates to." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
doubled = eval("2 * 21")
SRC
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get schema.wcl doubled
42
CONSOLE
  }

  p "`eval` parses and evaluates the expression **in the calling scope**, so the expression can see `self`, sibling fields and `let` bindings: a field written `doubled = eval(\"self.port * 2u32\")` inside a `service` whose `port` is `80u32` answers `160u32`."

  h3 "fn_signature"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(f: any) -> record
SRC
  }

  p "Describe a function's parameters and return type. Pass a function value, or a built-in's name as a string."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`f`" | "`any`" | "A function value, or the name of a built-in as a utf8 string." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`record`" | "A record `{ doc, params: [{name, type, doc}], return_type, return_doc, signature, is_builtin }`." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
map_sig = { let s = fn_signature("map"); s.signature }
SRC
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get schema.wcl map_sig
"fn(xs: [T], f: fn (T) -> U) -> [U]"
CONSOLE
  }

  p "Takes a function value, or a builtin's **name as a string**. The record it returns has to be bound before you can read a field off it: `fn_signature(\"map\").signature` fails with `expected a reference, got call`, while `{ let s = fn_signature(\"map\"); s.signature }` does not."

  h3 "namespace_decls"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(ns: utf8) -> [&T]
SRC
  }

  p "List references to every top-level declaration (`type` / `interface` / `union` / `symbol_set`) in a namespace, for schema-documentation generators. Pair with `decl_info`, `doc_comment`, `type_fields`, and `ast_string` to render each. Imported (library) declarations are included — filter on `decl_info(d).is_imported` to drop them."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`ns`" | "`utf8`" | "The namespace, dotted (e.g. `\"wdoc\"`); `\"\"` for the root namespace." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[&T]`" | "One reference per declaration: types first, then interfaces, unions, symbol sets, in source order." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
declarations = map(namespace_decls("app"), fn(t: &Service) -> utf8 { { let d = decl_info(t); d.name } })
SRC
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get schema.wcl declarations
["Service", "Limits", "Inventory"]
CONSOLE
  }

  p "Imported declarations are included. Filter on `decl_info(d).is_imported` to keep only the ones the document declares itself."

  h3 "type_fields"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(target: &T) -> [record]
SRC
  }

  p "Reflect a type or interface into a list of field-description records (own fields first, then inherited via `extends`)."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`target`" | "`&T`" | "A reference to a type or interface declaration." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`[record]`" | "One record per field: `{ name, type, is_function, optional, has_default, default, is_block, repeated, accepts, decorators }`." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
fields = map(type_fields(Service), fn(f: record) -> utf8 { f.name })
SRC
  }

  code console {
    source = <<'CONSOLE'
$ wcl get schema.wcl fields
["name", "port", "limits"]
CONSOLE
  }

  h2 "Paths and globs"

  p "Three pure string functions. Nothing here touches the disk. A path is a `/`-separated sequence of segments. Empty and `.` segments are dropped, and a pattern with a trailing `/` owns its whole subtree. `src`, `src/` and `./src` are the same path to all three."

  p "`glob_match` and `glob_overlaps` share one glob syntax: `*` matches within one segment, `**` spans whole segments, `?` matches one character, and `[abc]` / `[a-z]` / `[!x]` are character classes."

  h3 "glob_match"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(pattern: utf8, path: utf8) -> bool
SRC
  }

  p "Match one concrete path against a glob. `*` stays within a segment, `**` spans segments, `?` matches one character, `[a-z]` / `[!x]` are character classes. A trailing `/` on the pattern matches the whole subtree."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`pattern`" | "`utf8`" | "The glob pattern." |
      | "`path`" | "`utf8`" | "The concrete path to test." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`bool`" | "`true` if the path matches the pattern." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
glob_match("src/*.rs", "src/main.rs")   # → true
SRC
  }

  h3 "glob_overlaps"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(a: utf8, b: utf8) -> bool
SRC
  }

  p "Whether two glob patterns can match a common path. Concrete paths are patterns too, so this subsumes `glob_match` for overlap gates. Trailing `/` means the whole subtree. Conservative: exotic negated-class pairings may report `true` when no shared path exists, never `false` when one does."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`a`" | "`utf8`" | "The first glob pattern (or concrete path)." |
      | "`b`" | "`utf8`" | "The second glob pattern (or concrete path)." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`bool`" | "`true` if some path is matched by both patterns." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
glob_overlaps("src/", "src/*.rs")   # → true
SRC
  }

  p "Conservative in one direction only. An exotic pairing of negated classes may answer `true` when no shared path exists, but never `false` when one does."

  h3 "path_contains"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(parent: utf8, child: utf8) -> bool
SRC
  }

  p "Segment-aware path prefix test: whether `child` is `parent` itself or lives under it. Splits on `/`, so `src/` does not contain `src2/x`. A path contains itself."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`parent`" | "`utf8`" | "The containing path (trailing slash optional)." |
      | "`child`" | "`utf8`" | "The path to test." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`bool`" | "`true` if `child` equals `parent` or is nested beneath it." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
path_contains("src/", "src/core/mod.rs")   # → true
SRC
  }

  p "Segment-aware, so `src` does not contain `src2/x`. A path contains itself."

  h2 "Units"

  p "Two builtins, both going the same direction: from a stored base-unit number back to a labelled string. Writing `512MiB` in a document is the other direction, and the evaluator does that one. See [Values and primitives](lang_values#literal-units)."

  h3 "format_unit"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(value: i64, type: utf8, unit: utf8) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "Render a base-unit value in a chosen unit, looking the factor up from a unit type by name: `format_unit(size, \"std.ByteSize\", \"MiB\")` → `\"5 MiB\"`. The inverse of literal-unit resolution, so it stays correct if the type's `@unit` factor changes."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`value`" | "`i64`" | "The stored value, in the type's base unit." |
      | "`type`" | "`utf8`" | "The unit type's dotted name, e.g. `\"std.ByteSize\"`." |
      | "`unit`" | "`utf8`" | "The unit to render in, e.g. `\"MiB\"`." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "The value divided by the unit's factor, suffixed with the unit (e.g. `\"5 MiB\"`)." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
format_unit(5242880, "std.ByteSize", "MiB")   # → "5 MiB"
SRC
  }

  p "Reads the factor off the named type's `@unit` decorator rather than hard-coding one, so it stays correct if the factor changes. The exact inverse of writing `5MiB`. See [Values and primitives](lang_values#literal-units)."

  h3 "format_unit_value"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(value: i64, factor: i64, unit: utf8) -> utf8
SRC
  }

  p "Render a number in a unit given its factor explicitly: `format_unit_value(5242880, 1048576, \"MiB\")` → `\"5 MiB\"`. The primitive behind `format_unit` for callers that already hold the factor."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`value`" | "`i64`" | "The stored value, in the type's base unit." |
      | "`factor`" | "`i64`" | "The unit's multiplier (base units per one unit)." |
      | "`unit`" | "`utf8`" | "The unit label to append." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`utf8`" | "`value / factor` followed by the unit label." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
format_unit_value(5242880, 1048576, "MiB")   # → "5 MiB"
SRC
  }

  p "The primitive behind [`format_unit`](#format-unit), for a caller that already holds the factor. Nothing checks that the factor matches any declared unit."

  h2 "Builtins the host adds"

  p "The two the `wcl` CLI registers on wdoc's behalf. They are not part of the language, they are not in `wcl_lang`, and a host embedding WCL without wdoc will not have them. But `builtin_names()` reports them under the CLI, so they are catalogued here rather than left unexplained."

  p "Nothing stops you adding your own. A host registers a Rust closure against a name and it becomes callable from every document that host opens. See the `wcl_lang` crate docs for `Environment::add_builtin` and `from_fn`."

  h3 "page_metadata"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
fn(ctx: TemplateCtx) -> PageMetadata
SRC
  }

  p "Return memoised reading order, neighbours and active TOC path plus authored heading metadata for the current template page. Site metadata is indexed once and no other page body is evaluated."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`ctx`" | "`TemplateCtx`" | "The current template context." |
      | "**Returns**" | "`PageMetadata`" | "Metadata for the current page." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
{ let m = page_metadata(c); m.next }   # → the next TOC entry, or none
SRC
  }

  p "wdoc's, not the language's. It reads the current template context and answers the page's reading order, neighbours, active TOC path and heading outline. The site index is memoised and no page body is lowered. See [Templates and layouts](wdoc_templates)."

  h3 "__wdoc_slot"

  code text {
    source = <<'SRC'
(no signature registered)
SRC
  }

  p "Internal. No documentation is registered for it."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Parameter" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "**Returns**" | "`—`" | "Nothing documented." |
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'SRC'
__wdoc_slot(c.slots, name, :blocks)   # → the blocks filling the named slot
SRC
  }

  p "wdoc's, and **internal**. It backs the template `slot` machinery and is the one builtin the CLI registers with no documented signature. That is why `fn_signature(\"__wdoc_slot\")` answers a record whose every field is empty. Treat it as private to the standard library."

  h2 "Where to go next"

  list {
    li "[Lists, tensors and records](lang_collections). The three collection values every builtin in the first four sections operates on."
    li "[Functions](lang_functions). The `fn(…) -> T { … }` literal you hand to `map`, `filter`, `fold` and the `_by` family."
    li "[Control flow](lang_control_flow). `try`/`catch`, which recovers the failure a builtin raises."
    li "[Expressions and operators](lang_expressions#calls). Call syntax, precedence, and the operators that do what a builtin would otherwise have to."
    li "[Decorators](lang_decorators). What the reflection builtins are reading when they answer a decorator name or argument."
    li "[The CLI](lang_cli). `wcl repl`, which is the fastest way to check what a builtin does."
  }
}
